                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1027,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1016,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Makes copies of the whole tensor and lays them end to end, along each dimension - tiling.\n\nUse it to fill a larger grid with a pattern or to make a batch of identical copies. It is t.repeat with one difference: t.tile accepts fewer counts than the tensor has dimensions.\n\nEach count says how many copies to make along one dimension, so t.tile 3 2 on a 7 x 6 tensor stacks 3 copies down and\n2 across: 21 x 12. This is NOT numpy's repeat, which copies each element next to itself (0 0 1 1 2 2); here the WHOLE tensor is laid end to end (0 1 2 0 1 2).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.tile <count: int> <count: int> ...\nUsually one count per dimension (see the counts field below). With no\narguments the field is empty and the tensor is passed through\nunchanged.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.tile 3 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the copies on arrival. Only this inlet sends.\n\ntiling (text field, also an inlet):\nThe counts, separated by commas or spaces. Fewer counts than the tensor has dimensions are filled out with 1s at the FRONT, so the counts you give apply to the last dimensions: 2 on a 7 x 6 tensor means 1, 2 and doubles its width (7 x 12). (t.repeat refuses fewer counts.) More counts than dimensions add new dimensions at the front: 2, 1, 1 on a 7 x 6 tensor gives 2 x 7 x 6, two copies stacked along a new first dimension. A count of 1 leaves that dimension alone; 0 empties it. The next tensor uses a change - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntiled tensor out:\nThe copies, as one new tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 x 6, left) tiled 3, 2 gives 21 x 12 on the right - 3 rows of 2 F's. Type other counts: 1, 4 is a row of four, 3, 1 a column of three, and a single 2 is two side by side. t.info shows the shape of the result.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1000,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Makes copies of the whole tensor and lays them end to end, along each dimension - tiling.\n\nUse it to fill a larger grid with a pattern or to make a batch of identical copies. It is t.repeat with one difference: t.tile accepts fewer counts than the tensor has dimensions.\n\nEach count says how many copies to make along one dimension, so t.tile 3 2 on a 7 x 6 tensor stacks 3 copies down and\n2 across: 21 x 12. This is NOT numpy's repeat, which copies each element next to itself (0 0 1 1 2 2); here the WHOLE tensor is laid end to end (0 1 2 0 1 2).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.tile <count: int> <count: int> ...\nUsually one count per dimension (see the counts field below). With no\narguments the field is empty and the tensor is passed through\nunchanged.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.tile 3 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the copies on arrival. Only this inlet sends.\n\ntiling (text field, also an inlet):\nThe counts, separated by commas or spaces. Fewer counts than the tensor has dimensions are filled out with 1s at the FRONT, so the counts you give apply to the last dimensions: 2 on a 7 x 6 tensor means 1, 2 and doubles its width (7 x 12). (t.repeat refuses fewer counts.) More counts than dimensions add new dimensions at the front: 2, 1, 1 on a 7 x 6 tensor gives 2 x 7 x 6, two copies stacked along a new first dimension. A count of 1 leaves that dimension alone; 0 empties it. The next tensor uses a change - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntiled tensor out:\nThe copies, as one new tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 x 6, left) tiled 3, 2 gives 21 x 12 on the right - 3 rows of 2 F's. Type other counts: 1, 4 is a row of four, 3, 1 a column of three, and a single 2 is two side by side. t.info shows the shape of the result."
                },
